WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.520
Building new protein burns amino acids and a large share of cellular energy.

2
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.260
So how does a cell decide it can afford to grow?

3
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.160
Starting without permission or raw materials wastes scarce supplies.

4
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:21.480
One central kinase complex, mTORC1, makes that call.

5
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:26.433
Before this kinase switches on, it checks two separate questions.

6
00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:31.613
First, has the body sent a growth factor saying it is time to grow?

7
00:00:32.873 --> 00:00:36.393
Second, are there enough amino acids on hand to build protein?

8
00:00:37.673 --> 00:00:41.753
Two independent tracks carry those two answers straight to the lysosome.

9
00:00:42.733 --> 00:00:44.933
Follow the growth-factor track first.

10
00:00:46.153 --> 00:00:51.193
A signal such as insulin activates the kinase Akt at the plasma membrane.

11
00:00:52.493 --> 00:00:55.773
On the lysosome sits a small switch protein called Rheb.

12
00:00:56.853 --> 00:01:02.093
The TSC complex holds it off by making Rheb cut its GTP to GDP.

13
00:01:03.673 --> 00:01:07.813
Akt phosphorylates TSC, lifting that brake right off Rheb.

14
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:12.927
With the TSC brake lifted, Rheb stays loaded with GTP.

15
00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:17.807
Active Rheb is the direct activator of mTORC1.

16
00:01:19.207 --> 00:01:20.407
Notice where Rheb sits, though.

17
00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:26.167
A lipid tail anchors Rheb to internal membranes, including the lysosome.

18
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:30.807
Meanwhile, the kinase complex still drifts out in the cytoplasm.

19
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:34.007
Now follow the amino acid track.

20
00:01:35.287 --> 00:01:40.807
Another pair of small switches, the Rag proteins, sit anchored on that same lysosome

21
00:01:40.807 --> 00:01:45.187
surface. The Rags do not activate the kinase directly.

22
00:01:46.627 --> 00:01:52.827
Instead, when amino acids rise, the Rags grab mTORC1 and pull it onto the lysosome.

23
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:58.120
Separating activation from delivery builds a strict cellular gate.

24
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.780
Test it yourself. Suppose amino acids are plentiful, so the Rags hold the complex

25
00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.160
right on the lysosome.

26
00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:10.900
Now remove the growth factor from outside the cell.

27
00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.100
Does the kinase switch on, or stay off?

28
00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:27.613
It stays off. Without the growth factor, Akt quiets down and the TSC brake returns

29
00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:33.773
to the lysosome. TSC drains Rheb of GTP, turning that activator off.

30
00:02:35.093 --> 00:02:40.633
Even sitting right beside Rheb, docked mTORC1 stays silent without an active

31
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:41.633
partner.

32
00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:46.253
Now restore the growth factor while amino acids remain plentiful.

33
00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:54.193
Akt clears the TSC brake, Rheb lights up with GTP, and docked mTORC1 fires.

34
00:02:55.493 --> 00:02:59.013
Once active, it speeds up protein building and lipid synthesis.

35
00:02:59.773 --> 00:03:04.773
At the same time, it holds off cellular self-digestion, called autophagy.

36
00:03:05.817 --> 00:03:10.997
So mTORC1 needs both growth factors and amino acids to switch on.

37
00:03:12.337 --> 00:03:16.957
Growth factors activate Rheb, and amino acids deliver the kinase to meet it.

38
00:03:18.077 --> 00:03:22.677
This is the standard model, though some nutrients like glutamine can bypass the

39
00:03:22.677 --> 00:03:27.677
Rags. In normal cells, growth waits until both signals agree.
